module gunbc.fleet.fleet_naming_policy

// THE FLEET NAMING-POLICY REGISTRY: THE ONE ROW THAT DECIDES WHAT A MEMBER IS CALLED.
//
// WHY A REGISTRY RATHER THAN AN INLINE RULE. gunbc.plans.fleet_naming_reach_access P2 requires that
// "every hostname construction passes through a policy that names the allocation". What a member is
// called is a FACT ABOUT THE FLEET -- it is what makes a name legal -- and such a fact belongs in the
// ledger beside the roster it governs, not inside the function that reads it. Two consumers that each
// spell the rule inline can disagree silently; one row cannot. This is the same reason the roster itself
// is a `data` row in gunbc.hostname_allocation rather than a convention every caller remembers.
//
// WHY THESE COLUMNS ARE THE ATOMS. The DFS for this concept (the plan's section 3.2) is: a member's name
// is decided by (a) WHICH SPELLING of a drawn name is canonical, (b) WHAT a selector may spell in
// addition to the canonical name, (c) HOW the canonical name is qualified when it goes to a provider,
// and (d) WHETHER legacy rows may keep their stored spelling. Those four are the complete set: remove
// one and a consumer must invent it, add one and it is a decision nobody currently makes. The zone is NOT
// a fifth concept -- it is an input to (c), and it is the DNS zone type that already exists
// (extdeps.dns.domain_name DomainName), so this module borrows it rather than re-declaring a zone of its
// own.
//
// WHAT THIS ROW IS NOT: it is not a second roster and holds no per-host fact. Per-host truth stays in
// gunbc.hostname_allocation's roster, where allocation identity lives. A policy row that named a single
// host would be a second source for that host's name, which is the defect DESIGN 3 forbids.

import std.types { Bool, Int, NonEmptyStr, String, brand }
import std.optional { Absent, Optional, Present }
import extdeps.dns.domain_name { DomainName }

// THE REGISTRY'S IDENTITY, so a second regime (a lab fleet with its own naming rules) is a new ROW here
// rather than a second mechanism, and so a consumer can cite WHICH regime a name was resolved under.
type FleetNamingPolicyId = NonEmptyStr where brand("FleetNamingPolicyId")

// WHICH SPELLING OF A DRAWN NAME IS CANONICAL. These three are the only spellings a
// gunbc.hostname_allocation HumanName has at all -- they are the variant's own parts -- so a policy cannot
// ask for a form that does not exist and the enacting module never has to invent one. The naming is
// Canonical-prefixed because the column decides which spelling is the canonical one; the alternatives are
// the spellings themselves, not competing notions of canonicity.
type MemberLabelSource
  = CanonicalFirstOnly
  | CanonicalFirstLast
  | CanonicalFirstMiddleLast

// WHAT A SELECTOR MAY SPELL, beyond the canonical label. A policy column rather than an always-on
// convenience because the ambiguity rule it enables -- a short form resolves only when it picks out
// exactly one host -- is what keeps the convenience from becoming a collision: shortened forms are
// offered precisely so that one can be refused when it stops being unique. CanonicalOnly is the regime
// where the fleet accepts no abbreviations at all.
type SelectorFormPermission
  = CanonicalOnly
  | ShortenedFormsPermitted

// HOW THE CANONICAL LABEL IS QUALIFIED ON THE WAY TO A PROVIDER. The distinction the fleet actually makes
// is between a name that is complete in itself and one that needs the fleet's DNS zone appended. WHO
// resolves the qualification -- the fleet's DNS, the provider's nameserver -- is a provider fact and is
// deliberately not modeled here; that is the plan's point about a host living in two namespaces at once.
type MemberLabelQualification
  = BareLabel
  | QualifiedByFleetZone

// THE POLICY ROW. sole_constructor, for the same reason the identity types in gunbc.fleet.fleet_naming
// are: the shape is public so the row reads as data, but nothing outside this module may mint a policy.
// Every decision column is a sum of named alternatives, so a policy cannot be spelled as a string an `if`
// compares against, and a new regime has to say which of the named alternatives it picks.
//
// `legacy_labels_stay` is not bookkeeping; it is the migration decision, and the plan fixes it (existing
// LegacyHostname rows stay, the operator migrates them later). A consumer needs it because the fleet
// CONTAINS such rows today. Stated as a column, "this consumer must handle legacy rows" follows from the
// row rather than from each author's memory, and flipping the column later retires those code paths
// instead of re-opening them one call site at a time.
//
// `qualification_zone` is Optional for the same reason the qualification column exists: a fleet that
// speaks bare names has no zone to carry, and one that does must carry the REAL zone. An empty or
// placeholder zone would be worse than an absent one -- it would render as an address that looks valid.
type FleetNamingPolicy sole_constructor {
  id: FleetNamingPolicyId
  member_label_source: MemberLabelSource
  selector_forms: SelectorFormPermission
  member_label_qualification: MemberLabelQualification
  qualification_zone: Optional<DomainName>
  legacy_labels_stay: Bool
}

// THE ROW THE FLEET RUNS UNDER. Recorded, not derived: it states the regime the fleet actually uses, and
// the roster it governs is the evidence -- the two must agree, which is what
// dag/test/claim/fleet_naming_witness.dag checks instead of trusting this comment.
//
// CanonicalFirstOnly because it is what the roster's drawn rows actually are: the allocator's lottery
// yields the first untaken form of a ticket, and for every drawn member that form has been the given name
// alone (damien, gus, and the five P2 rows). A policy that selected a surname spelling would refuse every
// one of those rows, which is exactly what dag/test/claim/fleet_naming_witness.dag checks -- the row is
// recorded against the roster rather than asserted. The collision worry that argues for a surname is
// answered where it belongs: at the draw, which advances past a taken form rather than minting a duplicate.
// ShortenedFormsPermitted because the operator has always typed short names at a keyboard, and the
// roster's selector cardinality rule is what makes that safe. BareLabel because every host this fleet
// addresses today is addressed inside one network, so a suffix would be a second spelling of a host that
// already has one -- and with no zone declared, the qualification column and the zone field agree rather
// than one of them pretending.
//
// legacy_labels_stay is true because the fleet still contains legacy rows and the plan forbids
// withdrawing them to make a roster look tidier; it is the column P3 acts on, not a comment.
data gunbc_fleet_naming_policy: FleetNamingPolicy = FleetNamingPolicy {
  id: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_naming_policy.p2" as FleetNamingPolicyId,
  member_label_source: CanonicalFirstOnly,
  selector_forms: ShortenedFormsPermitted,
  member_label_qualification: BareLabel,
  qualification_zone: Absent,
  legacy_labels_stay: true,
}

// THE ONLY WAY THE POLICY IS READ: a function, so the row has one accessor, a consumer cannot hold a
// stale copy, and the module that enacts the policy has a single name to cite. This is what
// gunbc.fleet.fleet_naming calls.
fn select_fleet_naming_policy() -> FleetNamingPolicy {
  gunbc_fleet_naming_policy
}

// THE AMBIGUITY RULE, held beside the column that permits the shortcut. A selector form is accepted when
// the policy permits shortened forms AT ALL and the form picks out exactly one host: a count of zero is
// no such host and a count above one is ambiguous, and the plan's sentence is that both are refusals with
// no implicit winner. The caller supplies the count because only the roster knows how many hosts a form
// matches -- what lives here is the rule, not an index.
fn selector_form_is_accepted(permission: SelectorFormPermission, matches: Int) -> Bool {
  permission == ShortenedFormsPermitted && matches == 1
}

// Whether a legacy row may keep its stored spelling under this policy. Named as a question so the call
// site in gunbc.fleet.fleet_naming reads as the policy decision it is rather than a boolean field read.
fn legacy_labels_may_stay(policy: FleetNamingPolicy) -> Bool {
  policy.legacy_labels_stay
}

// The zone a qualified address is built with, if the policy carries one. Absent is a legitimate answer
// (a bare-name fleet) and the enacting module turns it into a refusal when the qualification column
// demands a zone -- this function does not substitute a zone it was not given.
fn policy_qualification_zone(policy: FleetNamingPolicy) -> Optional<DomainName> {
  policy.qualification_zone
}
